{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>These are the generations of Jacob: Joseph.</b> It should have mentioned all his generations, but by way of the plain sense (peshat) it subsumed all his generations under Joseph, to indicate that he encompassed every virtue of the rest of his brothers. And so the Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: all the virtues of the tribes' character traits were in him. The birthright of Reuben, of which it is said (I Chronicles 5:1): ״ובחללו יצועי אביו נתנה בכורתו ליוסף״ — and when he defiled his father's bed, his birthright was given to Joseph. The kingship of Judah, of which it is said (Genesis 42:6): ״ויוסף הוא השליט על הארץ״. The prophecy of Levi (ibid. 41): ״ויהי כאשר פתר לנו כן היה״. The wisdom of Issachar, of which it is said (ibid.): ״אין נבון וחכם כמוך״." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'These are the generations of Jacob: Joseph' — Scripture juxtaposed Joseph to Jacob because he resembled him, as it is said, 'for he is a child of his old age to him,' for the glow of his countenance was like his father's; and further, he resembled him in all his affairs. Just as Jacob was pursued by Esau his brother who sought to kill him, so Joseph was pursued by his brothers who sought to kill him. Just as Jacob's mother was barren, so Joseph's mother was barren. Just as Jacob was born circumcised, as it is said (Genesis 25:27): ״ויעקב איש תם״ — for one is not called תם unless circumcised, as it is said (ibid. 17:1): ״התהלך לפני והיה תמים״, so Joseph too was born circumcised, as it is said: ״יעקב יוסף״, that he was like him in every matter. Just as Jacob's mother had difficulty in childbirth, so Joseph's mother had difficulty in childbirth. Just as Jacob's mother bore two, so Joseph's mother bore two. Just as Jacob was a shepherd, so was Joseph. Just as Jacob took a wife outside the Land, so Joseph took a wife outside the Land and begot children there. Just as angels accompanied Jacob, so angels accompanied Joseph. Rabbi Yannai said: three angels were paired with him, as it is said: ״וימצאהו איש״, ״וישאלהו האיש״, ״ויאמר האיש״ — three times 'the man.' Just as Jacob was elevated through a dream, so Joseph was elevated through a dream. Just as Jacob's household, Laban's, was blessed on his account, so Joseph's household, Potiphar's, was blessed on his account. Just as Jacob went down to Egypt and made an end of the famine, so Joseph went down to Egypt and made an end of the famine. Just as Jacob died in Egypt, so Joseph died in Egypt. Just as Jacob was embalmed, so Joseph was embalmed. Just as Jacob's bones were brought up, so Joseph's bones were brought up." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> 'Jacob, Joseph' — Jacob is the cherub and so is Joseph, and this is what is said: 'the greater face and the lesser face,' and each one of them is called נער, as it is written (Hosea 11:1): ״כי נער ישראל ואהבהו״, and it is written of Joseph: ״והוא נער״." "<b>And Joseph came.</b> Every expression of bringing an evil report (הבאת דבה) that it uses means truth, for he brings proof for his words; whereas every expression of putting out an evil report (הוצאת דבה) means falsehood, one who puts out false words from his heart, in the manner said of the spies (Numbers 13:32): ״ויוציאו דבת הארץ רעה״. And it is written (Proverbs 10:18–19): ״מוציא דבה הוא כסיל״. And they are words of falsehood, in the manner of (Job 8:10): ״ומלבם יוציאו מילים״." "<b>Their evil report.</b> It is known that every report is evil, but it says רעה to intensify. <b>Their evil report.</b> Rashi of blessed memory explained: every fault that he saw in his brothers, the sons of Leah, he would report to his father — that they ate a limb from a living animal, and that they demeaned the sons of the maidservants by calling them slaves, and that they were suspect of sexual immorality; and he was punished for all three. And the Ramban wrote: if so, it would have been fitting for the sons of the maidservants to rescue Joseph from his brothers' hand, since he loved them and was zealous for their honor and would come on their behalf against the sons of Leah to report their misdeeds to his father. And if one should say that the sons of the maidservants were afraid of them, that is impossible, for they were four — Dan and Naphtali, Gad and Asher — and Reuben was with them, and Joseph himself; that makes six against the rest of the sons of Leah, and we see for certain that all the brothers agreed to sell him, except for Reuben the eldest of all and Benjamin the youngest of all. Therefore he of blessed memory explained that it was about the sons of the maidservants that he brought the report, and they are the sons of Bilhah and the sons of Zilpah, as mentioned in the verse; and the construal of the verse will be as if it said: 'These are the generations of Jacob: Joseph, being a lad of seventeen years, was shepherding his brothers with the flock' — these being the sons of Bilhah and the sons of Zilpah, his father's wives — 'and Joseph came with their evil report,' that of the sons of the maidservants mentioned. And afterward it says: ״ויראו אחיו״ — 'and his brothers saw,' these being the sons of Leah — 'that his father loved him more than all his brothers, and they hated him.' And so Joseph was hated by them all: by the sons of Bilhah and the sons of Zilpah because of the report he brought to their father, and by the sons of Leah because Jacob loved him more than them, and they were sons of the mistress just as he was."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {0 "The citation marked simply (שם מא) refers to Genesis 41; 'ibid.' relative to the prior Genesis reference."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:18:22.641950023Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}